.TH I2CHID 4
.SH NAME
i2chid \- touchpads and touch screens on I2C (HID over I2C)
.SH SYNOPSIS
No port: it sends what it reads to the console.
x86-64 only.
.B /bin/i2chid
is started at boot by
.BR init (8),
with device rights; with no I2C HID device it ends with status 0 and is not
started again.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B i2chid
drives up to two devices of a laptop (a 2-in-1 has a touchpad and a touch
screen):
.IP 1. 4
The firmware's ACPI tables
.RB ( sys_acpi (2))
say where each device is: a device "PNP0C50", its I2C address and speed,
the I2C controller, its interrupt pin and its HID descriptor's register.
.IP 2. 4
The controller: Intel's LPSS ("Serial IO") I2C, a Synopsys DesignWare core,
powered up and out of reset, a master at the bus's speed.
.IP 3. 4
HID over I2C: the HID descriptor, power on, reset, the report descriptor
(user/lib/hidrep.c reads it). A precision touchpad is switched to its own
mode (fingers), else it stays a mouse; a touch screen to its multi-touch
mode, its first finger then an absolute pointer.
.IP 4. 4
Reports: when the device pulls its interrupt pin low, it has one; it is
read and its moves and clicks go to the console
.RB ( CON_INJECT ,
like a USB mouse or tablet). Without the pin, or if interrupts do not come,
the device is read every 8 ms.
.PP
Everything is logged (the serial console,
.BR dmesg (1)),
so a laptop's log says what was found and where it stopped.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR con (4),
.BR acpid (4),
.BR ipts (4)
.SH BUGS
Two devices on one controller: only the first.
